.TH RECENSE 1 "2026-09-02" "senechal" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
recense \- census what is reachable from a prompt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" bashify: norun
.B recense where
\fINAME\fR
.br
.B recense paths
.br
.B recense
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B recense
answers one question: of everything a name could refer to, what actually
resolves off
.I $PATH
right now. It changes nothing \- every subcommand is read\-only, so its own
answer is never a side effect of asking it.
.PP
Bare, with no subcommand,
.B recense
walks every directory on
.IR $PATH ,
in order, and lists each executable regular file it finds \- first match per
name wins, the same precedence a shell already uses to resolve one. A
directory that is missing or unreadable is skipped for the count, not fatal
to it; the census still finishes and reports what it could see.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B (none)
Census every name reachable from a prompt. Exits
.BR 0 ,
including when the count is zero \- that is a real, correct answer.
.TP
.BI where " NAME"
Report the one path
.I NAME
resolves to, or state that it does not resolve at all. Exits
.B 0
if found,
.B 9
if not, and
.B 2
if
.I NAME
is missing.
.TP
.B paths
List each directory on
.I $PATH ,
one per line, in resolution order. A directory that exists but cannot be
read is the one thing this verb cannot see through \- reported as
.B BLIND
.RB ( 6 ),
not skipped, because a broken
.I $PATH
entry is exactly the drift this verb exists to surface.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output for the census and
.B where
subcommands.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress commentary; results only.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage and the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version.
.PP
.B recense
cannot spend money. It has no \fB\-\-summon\fR flag \- there is no action
here a mechanism cannot already perform, so there is nothing to escalate.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested answer was produced, including a census or a
.B where
lookup that legitimately found nothing.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error \- an unknown subcommand, an unknown flag, or
.B where
called with no
.IR NAME .
.TP
.B 6
BLIND \- a
.I $PATH
entry exists but could not be read, so
.B paths
cannot fully answer.
.TP
.B 9
.B where
ran and the name does not resolve to anything on
.IR $PATH .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
$ recense
$ recense where installe
$ recense paths
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR which (1),
.BR type (1),
.BR installe (1)
\- the verb that puts a name on
.I $PATH
in the first place;
.B recense where
is how a caller checks that it landed.
